  {
    "title": "Case Study: AI-Assisted Document Extraction with Human-in-the-Loop Review",
    "cat": "Implementation Practice",
    "body": [
      "Logistics receives commercial invoices and packing lists as PDFs from multiple suppliers. Their layouts, languages, scan quality and page structures vary. The project team proposes OCR and AI extraction for invoice number, purchase order, carton count, quantity, weight and issue date to reduce manual keying. Treat the proposal as a controlled document-processing workflow, not as an autonomous posting system.",
      "Classify document types and define the fields before selecting a tool. For every field, record its data type, expected format, authoritative verification source, business criticality and downstream use. Totals may be checked arithmetically; an invoice entity may need to be checked against an approved supplier record; a PO reference may need to match the purchase order; declared quantities may need comparison with the packing list and shipment instructions.",
      "Build a representative test corpus covering suppliers, languages, templates, rotated pages, faint scans, stamps, handwritten annotations, multi-page tables, amended documents and duplicate submissions. Keep development and evaluation samples separate. Measure extraction performance by field and document class, and record both false acceptance and false rejection. A single overall accuracy score can conceal poor performance on high-impact fields.",
      "Design a human-in-the-loop flow: the model proposes a value and points to its source location; deterministic rules check format and cross-field consistency; a reviewer confirms, edits or rejects the proposal; and the system records the original output, final value, reviewer and reason. Critical fields such as legal entity, currency, quantity and customs-declaration data must not be released without the approval required by company policy.",
      "Control input and output data through approved services and role-based access. Confirm processing location, retention period, deletion arrangements, subprocessors, access logging and whether the provider may use submitted documents for model training. Define a fallback route for unreadable files, unsupported formats, low-confidence fields and service outages. The exercise is to select five fields that require confidence-based routing and specify the action for each uncertain result.",
      "**Scope and evidence definition.** For the case “AI-Assisted Document Extraction with Human-in-the-Loop Review,” define the transaction boundary before configuring extraction. Identify the factory, supplier, shipment, PO, document type, document revision and receiving date. Establish which document is authoritative for each field: the PO for ordered quantities and terms, the invoice for billed values, the packing list for packed quantities and carton details, and the approved supplier master for legal identity. Create a field dictionary containing field name, type, format, mandatory status, source of truth, permitted transformation and downstream destination. Separate document-level validity from field-level correctness; a readable PDF can still contain an incorrect or superseded value.",
      "**Workflow design and control gates.** Route files through registration, malware and format checks, document classification, page segmentation, OCR, field extraction, normalization, validation and human review. Preserve the source file and page coordinates so a reviewer can inspect the exact evidence behind a proposed value. Use deterministic checks where possible: invoice number presence, currency code validity, arithmetic totals, PO existence, supplier identity match, and quantity comparison across related documents. Confidence thresholds must be calibrated on representative evaluation data and approved by the process owner; do not invent one universal threshold for all fields. Route high-impact or conflicting values to mandatory review even when model confidence is high. Prevent duplicate processing with a stable document or transaction identifier and an idempotent posting control.",
      "**Exception handling, audit trail and acceptance.** Maintain queues for unreadable pages, unknown suppliers, unmatched POs, conflicting quantities, duplicate invoices and low-confidence extraction. The reviewer must be able to correct a value without overwriting the model output. Store the source document reference, extracted value, corrected value, evidence location, rule results, reviewer identity, timestamp and reason. Before production use, test supplier variation, amended documents, page-order changes, duplicate uploads, missing fields and system outages. Accept the workflow only when field-level performance, exception workload, processing time, auditability and downstream reconciliation meet documented business criteria. Monitor drift as suppliers change templates and periodically re-evaluate the model on newly approved samples."
    ],
    "deep": "AI-assisted document extraction in an apparel business should be treated as a controlled data-entry and verification process. OCR converts page images into text; layout analysis identifies tables and field locations; an extraction model proposes structured values; validation rules compare those values with business records; and a reviewer resolves uncertainty. These stages have different failure modes. OCR may confuse characters such as 0/O or 1/I, layout parsing may associate a quantity with the wrong row, and semantic extraction may select a delivery address instead of the legal billing entity. A confidence score does not prove that a value is correct or authoritative.\n\n**Scope and evidence definition.** For the case “AI-Assisted Document Extraction with Human-in-the-Loop Review,” define the transaction boundary before configuring extraction. Identify the factory, supplier, shipment, PO, document type, document revision and receiving date. Establish which document is authoritative for each field: the PO for ordered quantities and terms, the invoice for billed values, the packing list for packed quantities and carton details, and the approved supplier master for legal identity. Create a field dictionary containing field name, type, format, mandatory status, source of truth, permitted transformation and downstream destination. Separate document-level validity from field-level correctness; a readable PDF can still contain an incorrect or superseded value.\n\n**Workflow design and control gates.** Route files through registration, malware and format checks, document classification, page segmentation, OCR, field extraction, normalization, validation and human review. Preserve the source file and page coordinates so a reviewer can inspect the exact evidence behind a proposed value. Use deterministic checks where possible: invoice number presence, currency code validity, arithmetic totals, PO existence, supplier identity match, and quantity comparison across related documents. Confidence thresholds must be calibrated on representative evaluation data and approved by the process owner; do not invent one universal threshold for all fields. Route high-impact or conflicting values to mandatory review even when model confidence is high. Prevent duplicate processing with a stable document or transaction identifier and an idempotent posting control.\n\n**Exception handling, audit trail and acceptance.** Maintain queues for unreadable pages, unknown suppliers, unmatched POs, conflicting quantities, duplicate invoices and low-confidence extraction. The reviewer must be able to correct a value without overwriting the model output. Store the source document reference, extracted value, corrected value, evidence location, rule results, reviewer identity, timestamp and reason. Before production use, test supplier variation, amended documents, page-order changes, duplicate uploads, missing fields and system outages. Accept the workflow only when field-level performance, exception workload, processing time, auditability and downstream reconciliation meet documented business criteria. Monitor drift as suppliers change templates and periodically re-evaluate the model on newly approved samples.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Build a field-level control matrix for invoice number, PO number, supplier legal name, currency, invoice total, carton count, packed quantity and gross weight. For each field, specify its authoritative source, validation rule, risk if wrong, reviewer requirement and exception route. Then test five document variants, including a poor scan and an amended invoice. Record extraction errors, corrections, review time and evidence retained. Do not authorize automatic financial posting or customs submission merely because the OCR output appears complete.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase20-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"AI document extraction with human review and controlled release\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>AI Document Extraction with Human-in-the-Loop Review</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#29364f\">Document extraction and controlled release</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Source evidence remains traceable through every decision</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">PDF source</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">and registration</text><path d=\"M148 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"164\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">OCR and</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">field extraction</text><path d=\"M298 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"314\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"380\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Rules and</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">cross-checks</text><path d=\"M448 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"464\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f7f1e9\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"530\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Human</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">review queue</text><path d=\"M598 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"76\" width=\"142\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eaf4ee\" stroke=\"#4c8b68\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"685\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Approved</text><text x=\"685\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">downstream use</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"171\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Keep source, model output, reviewer correction and approval evidence.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: Data Quality Investigation and Dashboard Reconciliation",
    "cat": "Implementation Practice",
    "body": [
      "A production dashboard reports 1,250 completed pieces for a day, while the line report records 1,180. Do not overwrite either total with a manually agreed figure. First establish whether both reports use the same definition of “completed,” the same cut-off time, factory scope, production lines, units of measure and treatment of rework or reversed transactions.",
      "Trace possible causes: duplicate scans, late event arrival, status reversals, garments transferred between lines, time-zone conversion, date filters, test records, unit conversion and different treatment of rework. Retain event ID, timestamp, factory, line, operation, style, order and source system for each record so that the difference can be reproduced at transaction level.",
      "Assess data quality across completeness, validity, consistency, timeliness and uniqueness. Define executable rules such as missing style identifiers, operations absent from the current routing, duplicate event IDs, impossible status transitions and events received outside the permitted processing window. Each rule needs an owner, severity, expected action and evidence of resolution.",
      "Create a data-quality issue log with the symptom, affected period and records, business impact, data owner, technical owner, suspected cause, confirmed cause, correction method, historical impact and prevention action. Never edit source transactions merely to make a dashboard agree with a manual report. Preserve the original value and an auditable correction or adjustment record.",
      "Close the incident only after the dashboard, source report and approved business definition reconcile. Save the reproduction steps and add regression tests for the cause found. The exercise is to design three automated checks for the discrepancy and decide which checks should raise an alert, quarantine records or block publication rather than silently rewrite source data.",
      "**Scope and reconciliation baseline.** For “Data Quality Investigation and Dashboard Reconciliation,” freeze the reporting period and capture the two original outputs before changing filters or refreshing data. Record the dashboard name and version, refresh timestamp, selected factory and line filters, KPI definition, report cut-off, source extracts and query or model version. Confirm whether 1,250 and 1,180 refer to pieces, operations, bundles or completed garments. Check whether one report counts scans while the other counts unique garments. Establish the exact population being compared and retain a reproducible sample of event identifiers.",
      "**Layer-by-layer diagnosis.** Reconcile the count from source transactions through ingestion, staging, transformation, semantic model and dashboard visual. At each layer, compare row counts, distinct event IDs, rejected records, duplicate records, date boundaries and aggregation logic. Inspect joins for one-to-many multiplication, late-arriving events, timezone shifts, null keys, changed routing definitions and status transitions. Classify each difference as a source capture issue, pipeline issue, transformation defect, KPI-definition mismatch or user-filter issue. Correct the defect at the layer where it originates; do not compensate in a later layer with an unexplained offset.",
      "**Correction, regression and closure evidence.** Assign a named owner and due date to each issue. If source data must be corrected, use an approved correction transaction with reason, approver and before/after values. Reprocess the affected period where required, reconcile totals against the authoritative line records, and confirm that unrelated days and lines have not changed. Add automated tests for uniqueness, referential integrity, allowed status transitions, event lateness and expected aggregation. Close only when the difference is explained, the correction is traceable, regression tests pass and the dashboard definition is documented for users."
    ],
    "deep": "A dashboard discrepancy is a symptom, not a diagnosis. The two totals may be calculated from different populations, cut-off times or event definitions. Even when both reports are technically correct according to their own rules, they may be unsuitable for direct comparison. The investigation must therefore combine business-definition review with technical lineage tracing.\n\n**Scope and reconciliation baseline.** For “Data Quality Investigation and Dashboard Reconciliation,” freeze the reporting period and capture the two original outputs before changing filters or refreshing data. Record the dashboard name and version, refresh timestamp, selected factory and line filters, KPI definition, report cut-off, source extracts and query or model version. Confirm whether 1,250 and 1,180 refer to pieces, operations, bundles or completed garments. Check whether one report counts scans while the other counts unique garments. Establish the exact population being compared and retain a reproducible sample of event identifiers.\n\n**Layer-by-layer diagnosis.** Reconcile the count from source transactions through ingestion, staging, transformation, semantic model and dashboard visual. At each layer, compare row counts, distinct event IDs, rejected records, duplicate records, date boundaries and aggregation logic. Inspect joins for one-to-many multiplication, late-arriving events, timezone shifts, null keys, changed routing definitions and status transitions. Classify each difference as a source capture issue, pipeline issue, transformation defect, KPI-definition mismatch or user-filter issue. Correct the defect at the layer where it originates; do not compensate in a later layer with an unexplained offset.\n\n**Correction, regression and closure evidence.** Assign a named owner and due date to each issue. If source data must be corrected, use an approved correction transaction with reason, approver and before/after values. Reprocess the affected period where required, reconcile totals against the authoritative line records, and confirm that unrelated days and lines have not changed. Add automated tests for uniqueness, referential integrity, allowed status transitions, event lateness and expected aggregation. Close only when the difference is explained, the correction is traceable, regression tests pass and the dashboard definition is documented for users.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Investigate the stated 70-piece difference. Create a reconciliation table with one row per cause hypothesis, the records or query needed to test it, expected evidence, owner and result. Demonstrate at least one duplicate-event check, one time-boundary check and one join-cardinality check. State which report is authoritative for the KPI and why. If the available evidence cannot explain all 70 pieces, leave the unresolved amount visible and document the remaining investigation rather than forcing the totals to match.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase20-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Trace a dashboard discrepancy from source events through the data pipeline to the published KPI\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Dashboard Discrepancy: Layer-by-Layer Reconciliation</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#29364f\">Reconcile the KPI from source to dashboard</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Compare records, definitions and transformations at each layer</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Source</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">events</text><path d=\"M148 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"164\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Ingestion</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">and staging</text><path d=\"M298 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"314\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"380\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Transform</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">and joins</text><path d=\"M448 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"464\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"530\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">KPI model</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">and filters</text><path d=\"M598 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"76\" width=\"142\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eaf4ee\" stroke=\"#4c8b68\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"685\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Reconciled</text><text x=\"685\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">dashboard</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"171\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Retain the original totals and explain every adjustment with evidence.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: MES Go-Live Readiness and Rollback Planning",
    "cat": "Implementation Practice",
    "body": [
      "A factory is preparing to launch an MES module on two sewing lines. The risk is not limited to software defects: master data, approved BOM revisions, scanning devices, network coverage, user readiness, SOPs, line-side support and the ability to return safely to the previous process all affect production continuity.",
      "Build a go-live readiness checklist covering approved process design; master data and BOM revision; user accounts and roles; devices and network; interfaces; migrated data; test cases; training; support coverage; backups; monitoring; downtime procedures and rollback. Every item needs an accountable owner, objective evidence, due date and status. A checked box without evidence is not a readiness confirmation.",
      "Set go/no-go criteria before the deployment window. Examples include unresolved critical defects, unreconciled transactions, untrained key users, an untested restore, unstable interfaces or missing line-side support. The authorized decision-maker must review the evidence and record the decision. Project schedule pressure is not a substitute for operational readiness.",
      "During hypercare, monitor login failures, missed scans, stuck transactions, data mismatches, response times and disruption to line rhythm. Provide a visible support channel, severity definitions, response targets agreed with the provider, escalation ownership and a method for recording workarounds. Review incidents at a cadence appropriate to the deployment risk.",
      "A rollback plan must define activation conditions, decision authority, how interfaces and synchronization will be stopped, how in-flight transactions will be handled, how records will be reconciled and what conditions permit a later return to the new system. Rehearse the plan in a suitable environment before go-live. Do not write the rollback procedure for the first time during a live incident.",
      "**Scope and cutover boundary.** For “MES Go-Live Readiness and Rollback Planning,” define the exact deployment scope: factory, two sewing lines, workstations, operations, users, devices, integrations and the production orders that may be processed during the cutover window. Identify the old and new systems of record for each transaction type and state when responsibility transfers. Freeze the approved configuration and master-data revisions for the cutover baseline. List excluded processes and manual fallback arrangements so that the deployment team does not assume that every factory function is moving at once.",
      "**Readiness evidence and go/no-go control.** Organize evidence into process, data, technology, security, people, support and continuity. Confirm that representative end-to-end scenarios have passed, including normal scans, rework, line transfer, downtime, duplicate submission, offline recovery and shift handover. Reconcile migrated opening balances and in-flight work against the source system. Verify user roles with actual line supervisors and operators, not only IT administrators. Confirm backup completion and perform a restore test. Record critical and high-severity defects, accepted workarounds, accountable risk owners and expiry dates. The go/no-go meeting should review evidence against criteria agreed before the deployment date.",
      "**Hypercare, rollback and controlled exit.** During hypercare, assign named business and technical leads to each shift. Track incidents by severity, affected line, transaction type, start time, workaround, production impact and resolution. Define rollback triggers such as loss of transaction integrity, prolonged unavailability beyond the approved tolerance or inability to reconcile production records. The rollback runbook must specify who authorizes it, how new transactions are paused, how queued events are handled, how the old process resumes and how duplicate or missing transactions are prevented. After stabilization, reconcile all transactions, close temporary access and workarounds, transfer open issues to normal support and complete a post-go-live review."
    ],
    "deep": "A successful MES go-live is a controlled operational cutover, not simply the moment the new application becomes accessible. The factory must be able to continue recording production events, identify the authoritative record, support users on every affected shift and recover without losing or duplicating transactions. The plan should be designed around production continuity and data integrity.\n\n**Scope and cutover boundary.** For “MES Go-Live Readiness and Rollback Planning,” define the exact deployment scope: factory, two sewing lines, workstations, operations, users, devices, integrations and the production orders that may be processed during the cutover window. Identify the old and new systems of record for each transaction type and state when responsibility transfers. Freeze the approved configuration and master-data revisions for the cutover baseline. List excluded processes and manual fallback arrangements so that the deployment team does not assume that every factory function is moving at once.\n\n**Readiness evidence and go/no-go control.** Organize evidence into process, data, technology, security, people, support and continuity. Confirm that representative end-to-end scenarios have passed, including normal scans, rework, line transfer, downtime, duplicate submission, offline recovery and shift handover. Reconcile migrated opening balances and in-flight work against the source system. Verify user roles with actual line supervisors and operators, not only IT administrators. Confirm backup completion and perform a restore test. Record critical and high-severity defects, accepted workarounds, accountable risk owners and expiry dates. The go/no-go meeting should review evidence against criteria agreed before the deployment date.\n\n**Hypercare, rollback and controlled exit.** During hypercare, assign named business and technical leads to each shift. Track incidents by severity, affected line, transaction type, start time, workaround, production impact and resolution. Define rollback triggers such as loss of transaction integrity, prolonged unavailability beyond the approved tolerance or inability to reconcile production records. The rollback runbook must specify who authorizes it, how new transactions are paused, how queued events are handled, how the old process resumes and how duplicate or missing transactions are prevented. After stabilization, reconcile all transactions, close temporary access and workarounds, transfer open issues to normal support and complete a post-go-live review.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Prepare a go/no-go pack for the two-line MES launch. Include a readiness matrix, defect list, migration reconciliation, shift support roster, deployment timeline, rollback decision tree and transaction-recovery procedure. Simulate a network outage during a shift and a case in which some scans reach MES while others remain queued. Show how the team prevents double counting when service returns. Record the decision authority and the evidence required to resume normal operation.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase20-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"MES go-live readiness, go or no-go decision, hypercare and rollback control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>MES Go-Live Readiness and Rollback Control</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#29364f\">Controlled MES cutover and recovery</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Readiness evidence gates deployment and protects production continuity</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Readiness</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">evidence</text><path d=\"M148 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"164\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Go / no-go</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">approval</text><path d=\"M298 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"314\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"380\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Cutover</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">and validation</text><path d=\"M448 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"464\" y=\"76\" width=\"132\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eef1f7\" stroke=\"#5b6e91\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"530\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Hypercare</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">and monitoring</text><path d=\"M598 109 h13 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"76\" width=\"142\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#eaf4ee\" stroke=\"#4c8b68\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"685\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#29364f\">Stable use</text><text x=\"685\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#29364f\">or controlled rollback</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"171\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Protect transaction integrity, line continuity and recovery evidence.</text></svg></figure>"
  }
